text:
At the end of it, a rambling Hindi voice note comes back as a two-line summary. Read aloud, in the same language.

It worked on the first try. That is exactly why I almost shipped it wrong. Twice.

The building was easy. Building it correctly was not. The difference came down to two behaviors the quickstart never mentions. You only find them by running the thing and reading what comes back.

What I built

I wanted a small, honest test of Sarvam's stack. So I built voxsar. It takes an Indic voice note and gives you the gist.

It chains three of Sarvam's APIs. Speech-to-text (Saaras) turns the audio into a transcript. The chat model (Sarvam 105B) summarizes it. Text-to-speech (Bulbul) reads the summary back in the language you spoke.

I gave myself one rule: zero npm dependencies. Node 18 and up already has fetch, FormData, and --env-file built in. So the whole thing is three small files and nothing to install. A full run costs about sixty paise. Every new Sarvam account starts with a hundred rupees of free credit, so I could run it a hundred times before paying anything.

The question I cared about was not "can you chain these three calls." You can. It is trivial. The real question is the one a quickstart cannot answer for you. What does it not tell you that you only learn by running it?

The gap the docs leave

Most API quickstarts have the same honest shape. Sarvam's included. They are built to make one call succeed. Copy the curl command, paste your key, get a 200 back, feel good. That is a fair goal for a quickstart. It is a bad goal for a pipeline.

A pipeline has to stay cheap, correct, and fast across many calls, on messy input. None of that shows up in a single happy-path example. It shows up when you instrument what you built and look at the numbers.

So I did. Two surprises fell out.

Gotcha one: the model is thinking when you did not ask it to

The first surprise was in the token count.

I sent Sarvam 105B a transcript. I asked for a one or two sentence summary. The summary came back correct and short, about twenty words. Then I looked at the usage block. 411 completion tokens.

Four hundred and eleven tokens to write twenty words. The model was reasoning before it answered. It spent roughly 390 invisible tokens working through a task that needs no working-through. Summarizing is not a puzzle. There is nothing to reason about.

Sarvam 105B has a reasoning mode. It is controlled by a reasoning_effort parameter, and it defaults to medium. You can set it to low or high. One line in the reference notes that you can switch it off entirely by setting it to None. For a summarize, extract, or classify task, off is what you want:

{
 model: 'sarvam-105b',
 messages,
 reasoning_effort: null,
 temperature: 0.2,
}

With reasoning off, the wasted tokens go away. So does the latency they were costing. On a summary the quality did not change, because there was never any reasoning worth doing.

Keep the effort up for genuine multi-step problems. Turn it off for the mechanical calls. In a pipeline like this, that is most of them.

That gotcha is about something you cannot see. The next one is about your own text, which you can see. If you look.

Gotcha two: codemix mode rewrites your English

Real Indian voice notes are not in one language. People switch mid-sentence. "Kal morning office jaldi pahunchna hai, phir client ke saath meeting hai." Hindi grammar, English nouns, one breath. Sarvam's speech-to-text has a codemix mode for exactly this. So I used it.

I expected the transcript to keep the English words as English. It does not. It transliterates them into Devanagari.

My note said "office," "meeting," "grocery store," "bread," and "presentation." The transcript came back with ऑफिस, मीटिंग, ग्रोसरी स्टोर, ब्रेड, and प्रेजेंटेशन. Nothing was dropped or garbled. Every word survived, spelled phonetically in the note's own script. The transcript is in one consistent script instead of two.

For my summarizer, that is the right behavior. A summary in a single script reads clean, and the meaning is fully intact. But it will surprise you if you assumed otherwise. And it matters the moment you do anything with the transcript besides read it.

Say you are keyword-matching on English terms. Or routing on a word like "invoice." Or showing the raw transcript to a user who typed in English. That "invoice" is now इनवॉइस, and your exact-match check fails. The fix is not code. The fix is knowing it happens and planning around it.

Build it yourself

None of this is hard to reproduce. I would rather you run it than take my word for it.

Get your free credit and key from the Sarvam dashboard. Put the key in a .env file. You need Node 18 or newer and nothing else.

The pipeline is three functions. Speech-to-text posts the audio as multipart form data, with mode set to codemix. The chat call sends the transcript with reasoning off and a system prompt that tells the model, firmly, to summarize in the note's language and never switch to English. Text-to-speech takes the summary and returns a base64 WAV. You decode it from the audios array and write it to disk.

One detail I am glad I added. A small helper generates the test voice note using Sarvam's own text-to-speech. So you can demo the whole tool without recording your own voice. Your test input is reproducible instead of a one-off clip. The machine talked to itself the entire time, and it made testing far easier.

Then run it and read the output. Watch the usage block. Watch the cost line, which the tool computes from the real audio seconds, tokens, and characters of each run. It does not guess. The two gotchas are what you notice once you are looking at real numbers instead of a single green checkmark.

The takeaway

It worked on the first try, and that was the trap. A quickstart proves the API responds. A real build tells you how it behaves. Behavior is where the cost and the correctness live.

Build the small thing. Measure it. The two things that matter show up on their own.

The full code

Three files. You need Node 18 or newer and a Sarvam API key in a .env file next to them. Make a test note with node --env-file=.env make-sample.mjs, then run node --env-file=.env voxsar.mjs sample-note.wav.

sarvam.mjs, the three API calls:

// Sarvam API calls, shared by voxsar.mjs and make-sample.mjs.
// Never logs the key. Throws clean Error messages (callers print one line + exit 1).
import { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';

const BASE = 'https://api.sarvam.ai';

export function apiKey() {
 const k = process.env.SARVAM_API_KEY;
 if (!k) throw new Error('SARVAM_API_KEY not set. Put it in .env and run with: node --env-file=.env');
 return k;
}

function headers(extra = {}) {
 return { 'api-subscription-key': apiKey(), ...extra };
}

async function post(path, init) {
 const r = await fetch(BASE + path, init);
 if (!r.ok) {
 const body = await r.text().catch(() => '');
 throw new Error(`Sarvam ${path} failed: HTTP ${r.status} ${body.slice(0, 200)}`);
 }
 return r.json();
}

// Text -> base64 WAV (16-bit mono 22050 Hz).
export async function tts(text, languageCode = 'hi-IN', speaker = 'shubh') {
 const json = await post('/text-to-speech', {
 method: 'POST',
 headers: headers({ 'Content-Type': 'application/json' }),
 body: JSON.stringify({ text, language_code: languageCode, model: 'bulbul:v3', speaker }),
 });
 const b64 = json.audios?.[0];
 if (!b64) throw new Error('TTS returned no audio');
 return b64;
}

// Audio file -> { transcript, languageCode, languageProbability }. codemix mode
// so Hindi+English notes transcribe intact.
export async function stt(filePath) {
 const buf = readFileSync(filePath);
 const fd = new FormData();
 fd.append('model', 'saaras:v3');
 fd.append('mode', 'codemix');
 fd.append('file', new Blob([buf], { type: 'audio/wav' }), 'audio.wav');
 const json = await post('/speech-to-text', { method: 'POST', headers: headers(), body: fd });
 return {
 transcript: json.transcript ?? '',
 languageCode: json.language_code ?? 'hi-IN',
 languageProbability: json.language_probability,
 };
}

// messages[] -> { content, usage }. Reasoning disabled (summaries need none);
// falls back to low effort if the API rejects a null reasoning_effort.
export async function chat(messages) {
 const call = (reasoning_effort) => post('/v1/chat/completions', {
 method: 'POST',
 headers: headers({ 'Content-Type': 'application/json' }),
 body: JSON.stringify({ model: 'sarvam-105b', messages, reasoning_effort, temperature: 0.2 }),
 });
 // reasoning disabled (null); retry at low effort only if the API rejects null.
 const json = await call(null).catch(() => call('low'));
 const content = json.choices?.[0]?.message?.content;
 if (!content) throw new Error('Chat returned no content');
 return { content, usage: json.usage ?? {} };
}

voxsar.mjs, the pipeline and the cost math:

// voxsar — Indic voice note -> transcript -> summary -> spoken summary (same language).
// Run: node --env-file=.env voxsar.mjs <audio-file>
import { readFileSync, writeFileSync, existsSync } from 'node:fs';
import { stt, chat, tts } from './sarvam.mjs';

// Sarvam pay-as-you-go rates (INR), from SPEC.md §7.
const RATE = { sttPerHour: 30, llmInPerM: 29.28, llmOutPerM: 73.2, ttsPer1000: 3 };

// Duration of a PCM WAV in seconds, from its byteRate + data-chunk size.
// Returns { seconds, exact }. exact=false when the file isn't a parseable WAV.
function wavSeconds(buf) {
 const isWav = buf.length > 44 && buf.toString('ascii', 0, 4) === 'RIFF' && buf.toString('ascii', 8, 12) === 'WAVE';
 if (!isWav) return { seconds: Math.max(1, (buf.length - 44) / 44100), exact: false };
 const byteRate = buf.readUInt32LE(28);
 const dataIdx = buf.indexOf('data', 12, 'ascii');
 const dataSize = dataIdx >= 0 ? buf.readUInt32LE(dataIdx + 4) : buf.length - 44;
 return { seconds: dataSize / byteRate, exact: true };
}

function costLine({ sttSec, sttExact, usage, ttsChars }) {
 const sttCost = (sttSec / 3600) * RATE.sttPerHour;
 const llmCost = ((usage.prompt_tokens ?? 0) / 1e6) * RATE.llmInPerM +
 ((usage.completion_tokens ?? 0) / 1e6) * RATE.llmOutPerM;
 const ttsCost = (ttsChars / 1000) * RATE.ttsPer1000;
 const total = sttCost + llmCost + ttsCost;
 const approx = sttExact ? '' : ' (STT approx — non-WAV input)';
 return `₹${total.toFixed(2)} this run${approx}`;
}

async function main() {
 const file = process.argv[2];
 if (!file) throw new Error('Usage: node --env-file=.env voxsar.mjs <audio-file>');
 if (!existsSync(file)) throw new Error(`File not found: ${file}`);

const buf = readFileSync(file);
 const { seconds: sttSec, exact: sttExact } = wavSeconds(buf);

const { transcript, languageCode, languageProbability } = await stt(file);
 if (!transcript.trim()) throw new Error('Transcript came back empty — is the audio valid speech?');

const { content: rawSummary, usage } = await chat([
 {
 role: 'system',
 content: 'You summarize a voice note in 1-2 short sentences. ' +
 'Write the summary in the SAME language and script as the note. ' +
 'Never translate to English if the note is not in English. ' +
 'Keep every key fact (names, times, tasks). Output only the summary.',
 },
 { role: 'user', content: transcript },
 ]);
 const summary = rawSummary.trim();

const b64 = await tts(summary, languageCode);
 const outPath = file.replace(/\.[^.]+$/, '') + '.summary.wav';
 writeFileSync(outPath, Buffer.from(b64, 'base64'));

const conf = languageProbability != null ? ` (confidence ${Number(languageProbability).toFixed(2)})` : '';
 console.log(`\n🎧 voxsar — ${file}`);
 console.log(`Detected language: ${languageCode}${conf}`);
 console.log('\n── Transcript ───────────────────────');
 console.log(transcript);
 console.log('\n── Summary ─────────────────────────');
 console.log(summary);
 console.log(`\n🔊 Spoken summary → ${outPath}`);
 console.log(`💸 ${costLine({ sttSec, sttExact, usage, ttsChars: summary.length })}\n`);
}

main().catch((e) => {
 console.error('Error:', e.message);
 process.exit(1);
});

make-sample.mjs, so you never have to record your own voice:

// Generate a synthetic voice note for testing voxsar — no human recording needed.
// Code-mixed Hindi+English, the way people actually leave voice notes.
// Run: node --env-file=.env make-sample.mjs
import { writeFileSync } from 'node:fs';
import { tts } from './sarvam.mjs';

const NOTE = 'कल morning मुझे office जल्दी पहुँचना है, फिर 3 बजे client के साथ एक ज़रूरी meeting है। ' +
 'शाम को grocery store से दूध, bread और थोड़े fruits ले आना। और हाँ, Rahul को call करके presentation के बारे में बताना मत भूलना।';

try {
 const b64 = await tts(NOTE, 'hi-IN');
 writeFileSync('sample-note.wav', Buffer.from(b64, 'base64'));
 console.log('Wrote sample-note.wav');
 console.log('Try: node --env-file=.env voxsar.mjs sample-note.wav');
} catch (e) {
 console.error('Error:', e.message);
 process.exit(1);
}

That is the whole thing. Three files, MIT-licensed, no dependencies. Copy them, drop your Sarvam key in a .env, and run it on your own notes. And if you are building anything with Indic voice, I want to hear which gotchas bit you. Trade notes with me.

